This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

```cs
using System;

class Program
{
    public event Action<string?>? E;

    static void Main()
    {
        var p = new Program();
        p.EWrapper += s => s.ToString();
        p.E?.Invoke(null); // No warning, but an NRE is reported at runtime

        // A similar case involving combining delegates produces warnings,
        // but only when compound assignment is not used
        Action<string> a1 = s => s.ToString();
        Action<string?> a2 = s => { };
        a2 = a1 + a2; // warning
        a2 += a1; // no warning
        a2(null);
    }

    public event Action<string>? EWrapper
    {
        add => E += value; // Probably, a nullable warning should be reported here, whether 'E' field is accessible or not
        remove => E -= value;
    }
}
```

**Version Used**: Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp `5.12.0-1.26467.104` (latest from the `dotnet-tools` feed). Also reproduces with `5.9.0` from nuget.org.

## Steps to Reproduce

Ask for the `IOperation` of a string concatenation where one operand is a `char` constant:

```csharp
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Syntax;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Operations;

var code = """
    class C
    {
        string M1() => "abc" + "d"; // string + string
        string M2() => "abc" + 'd'; // string + char: emitted as ldstr "abcd"
    }
    """;

var tree = CSharpSyntaxTree.ParseText(code);
var compilation = CSharpCompilation.Create("Test", [tree],
    [MetadataReference.CreateFromFile(typeof(object).Assembly.Location)],
    new CSharpCompilationOptions(OutputKind.DynamicallyLinkedLibrary));

var model = compilation.GetSemanticModel(tree);
foreach (var node in tree.GetRoot().DescendantNodes().OfType<BinaryExpressionSyntax>())
{
    var operation = (IBinaryOperation)model.GetOperation(node)!;
    Console.WriteLine($"{node}");
    Console.WriteLine($"  ConstantValue: {Format(operation.ConstantValue)}");
    Console.WriteLine($"  Left:  {operation.LeftOperand.Kind} ({operation.LeftOperand.Type}) ConstantValue: {Format(operation.LeftOperand.ConstantValue)}");
    Console.WriteLine($"  Right: {operation.RightOperand.Kind} ({operation.RightOperand.Type}) ConstantValue: {Format(operation.RightOperand.ConstantValue)}");
    if (operation.RightOperand is IConversionOperation conversion)
        Console.WriteLine($"    Operand: {conversion.Operand.Kind} ({conversion.Operand.Type}) ConstantValue: {Format(conversion.Operand.ConstantValue)}");
}

static string Format(Optional<object?> value) => value.HasValue ? $"\"{value.Value}\"" : "<none>";
```

Output:

```text
"abc" + "d"
  ConstantValue: "abcd"
  Left:  Literal (string) ConstantValue: "abc"
  Right: Literal (string) ConstantValue: "d"
"abc" + 'd'
  ConstantValue: <none>
  Left:  Literal (string) ConstantValue: "abc"
  Right: Conversion (object) ConstantValue: <none>
    Operand: Literal (char) ConstantValue: "d"
```

The compiler folds the same expression into a single constant when it emits code. `string M2() => "abc" + 'd';` compiles to:

```il
IL_0000: ldstr "abcd"
IL_0005: ret
```

## Expected Behavior

`IBinaryOperation.ConstantValue` for `"abc" + 'd'` is `"abcd"`, which is the value the compiler emits.

## Actual Behavior

`IBinaryOperation.ConstantValue` has no value. The right operand is an implicit boxing conversion to `object`, and that conversion has no constant value either, although its operand (the `'d'` literal) does.

## Notes

I understand that `"abc" + 'd'` is not a *constant expression* under the language rules: `const string S = "abc" + 'd';` reports `CS0133`. But lowering still folds it to a constant, so the compiler knows the value.

This matters for analyzers. An analyzer that checks whether a string argument is a constant (for example: a format string, a logging message template, a regex pattern, or a `[StringSyntax]` argument) has to special-case `string + char` and re-implement the folding itself. Otherwise it treats the argument as non-constant, which is inconsistent with the IL the compiler produces.

If changing `ConstantValue` is not an option because it must match the language's definition of constant expressions, could the folded value be exposed another way?

